/* book: lead: A held sample can take any value, but a converter stores it in a fixed number of bits, so it must round the sample to one of a finite set of levels. That rounding is quantization. The quantization error eq = Q(x) − x, the rounded value minus the true sample, behaves like a small added noise. That noise model links the number of bits to accuracy: it predicts the hiss of digital audio, the resolutio */
/* book Figure 5.4.1: Left: the 3-bit, 0 V to 8 V mid-rise quantizer climbs in steps of Δ = 1 V to the levels 0.5, 1.5, …, 7.5 V; the input 3.4 V is rounded to 3.5 V. Right: the error eq = Q(x) − x saws between +0.5 V and −0.5 V on every step and is +0.1 V at 3.4 V. */
